// NEGATIVE TEST -- a nested array parameter whose INNER length is a const
// generic is refused (N4-10).
//
// C decays an array parameter to a pointer to its first element, and for
// `[[i64; 2]; 3]` that element is a row of 2: `long long g[3][2]` IS
// `long long (*g)[2]`. So the outermost length may be left open -- C throws it
// away anyway, and this compiler already writes `long long xs[]` for a
// `[i64; N]` parameter -- but an inner one may not. It is the stride of `g[i]`,
// and there are only two ways to print a length this compiler cannot resolve:
// `[0]`, which is a wrong stride and would compute wrong addresses silently, or
// the const generic's own name, which is not declared in the generated C and
// reaches gcc as "use of undeclared identifier 'N'".
//
// Const generic lengths are DROPPED (language-spec.md §5), so `N` is not a
// number this pass can ever recover. A caller cannot reach this function --
// passing `[[i64; 2]; 3]` fails as "Type mismatch: expected [[Int; N]; 3]" --
// but a body is code-generated whether or not anything calls it (see
// tests/reject/uncalled_body_is_checked.pd), so this is the position where the
// declaration actually has to be written.
fn pick(g: [[i64; N]; 3]) -> i64 {
    return g[0][0];
}

fn main() {
    print_int(1);
}
